## Why This Is Asked

This question tests designing a simple, resilient per-tenant model routing with clear failover, suitable for beginners while demonstrating practical data modeling, API surface, and basic testing.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant config: pinnedModelVersion, fallbackVersion
- Health checks and routing logic
- Failover handling and latency observability
- Simple durable storage for configs

## Code Example

```javascript
function chooseModelVersion(tenantId, healthMap, configStore) {
  const cfg = configStore.get(tenantId);
  const ok = healthMap[cfg?.pinnedModelVersion];
  if (cfg && ok) return cfg.pinnedModelVersion;
  return cfg?.fallbackVersion;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test race conditions between health checks and routing?
- How would you handle cross-region config drift?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about lightweight, policy-driven routing in a multi-tenant LLM ops context. Focuses on practical data modeling, decision logic, and test coverage for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant routing state (budget, privacy)
- Latency estimation and budget checks
- Policy-compliant model selection and auditing
- Basic test plan with reproducible scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
function routeForTenant(tenant, models, estimates, budgets){
  // pick fastest model within budget and privacy constraints
  const allowed = models.filter(m => tenant.allowedModels.includes(m.name) && m.privacyOK === true);
  const feasible = allowed.filter(m => estimates[m.name] <= tenant.latencyBudgetMs);
  return feasible.sort((a,b) => estimates[a.name]-estimates[b.name])[0] || null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate burst traffic and verify SLA adherence?
- How would you adapt routing when a model becomes unavailable or budget changes mid-session?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a dynamic privacy policy system that balances latency, data locality, and cross-tenant isolation in a real-world multi-tenant LLM gateway.

Key Concepts

- Adaptive policy routing between local and centralized privacy services
- Per-tenant privacy budgets and policy versioning
- Canary rollouts and drift detection
- Data locality, auditability, and fault tolerance

Code Example

```typescript
type TenantPolicy = { id: string; useCentralService: boolean; budget: number; version: number };
function selectRoute(tenant: TenantPolicy, latencyMs: number): 'local'|'central' {
  if (!tenant.useCentralService) return 'local';
  if (tenant.budget <= 0) return 'local';
  return latencyMs < 140 ? 'local' : 'central';
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor policy drift and automatically rollback canaries?
- How would you model privacy budgets and ensure fairness across tenants?

